<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-chapter-8" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Line Integrals and Conservative Fields</title>

<introduction>
  <p>
    In this chapter, we introduce line integrals and vector fields, which allow us to measure quantities
    that accumulate along curves. Unlike earlier integrals over regions, line integrals depend on both
    the path taken and the direction of motion. Vector fields provide the setting for these ideas by
    assigning a vector to every point in space.
  </p>

  <p>
    We begin by defining scalar line integrals and interpreting them geometrically, including their
    connection to arc length. We then study vector line integrals, which model physical quantities such
    as work done by a force along a path. Parametric descriptions of curves play a central role, linking
    the geometry of a path to the algebra of integration.
  </p>

  <p>
    The chapter concludes with conservative vector fields and potential functions.
    When a vector field is conservative, line integrals simplify dramatically and depend only on
    endpoints rather than paths. By the end of the chapter, you should be able to recognize conservative
    fields, find potential functions, and use them to evaluate line integrals efficiently.
  </p>
</introduction>

